<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manbij</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hierapolis Bambyce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manbij is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. In antiquity it was Hierapolis Bambyce, a famous centre of the cult of the Syrian goddess Atargatis. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a fortified town contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>; the Artuqid emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Belek Ghazi</a> was killed besieging it in 1124.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manbij.jpg" alt="Manbij" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacky Lee · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_street_view_of_manbij_city_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
